rain-core: xray/0057-muxcool-soak-fault-injection
Source: ReClash test on top of xray/0049-0056 (GPL-3.0).

The hardening series fixed the mux.cool transport but had no test that could
produce the failures it fixes on demand: no fault-injecting carrier, no soak,
and no goroutine check after close. This patch adds that verification layer
and the two production defects it found.

- The carrier fault harness wraps net.Pipe in a net.Conn that injects
  latency, deterministic jitter, a token-bucket bandwidth limit, temporary
  write stalls, short writes, and a partial write failure; the dialer owns
  the peer side so a test can produce a clean FIN, a TCP reset (SO_LINGER 0)
  and a blackholed peer the way a real peer does.
- Fault coverage: TCP and UDP sessions at 0/20/250 ms delay and 20 ms of
  jitter; a write stalled past the frame timeout; short and partial writes;
  peer EOF and RST; a server that stops reading; sibling isolation.
- Goroutine leak detection without a new dependency: runtime.NumGoroutine
  before and after a mixed TCP+UDP workload with carrier rotation and
  deadline churn, bounded retry, runtime.Stack dump on growth.
- Opt-in soak behind MIHOMO_SOAK_SECONDS (MIHOMO_SOAK_WORKERS defaults to
  128): concurrent TCP and UDP sessions, open/close churn, rotation,
  ErrSessionOverflow, UDP drops and periodic carrier kills.

Two defects found and fixed:

1. A clean carrier EOF dropped downlink payload that had already been
   decoded and queued for a slow TCP consumer: session.closeCarrier closed
   the session immediately. A carrier FIN now queues the EOF behind the
   queued payload and lets runDownlink finish after the last write; resets
   and protocol errors keep the reset path. UDP stays lossy: queued datagrams
   are dropped on carrier close.
2. A carrier whose frame write had failed kept reporting itself available
   until the asynchronous close ran, so Pool.DialContext could return a
   session that was dead before its first byte. fail() now marks the worker
   failed synchronously and allocateIDLocked checks it.

Tests: TestFaultInjectionDelayedEcho, TestFaultInjectionBandwidthThrottle,
TestFaultInjectionWriteStallExceedsFrameTimeout,
TestFaultInjectionServerStopsReading, TestFaultInjectionShortWrites,
TestFaultInjectionPartialWriteFailureClosesCarrier,
TestFaultInjectionPeerEOF, TestFaultInjectionPeerReset,
TestFaultInjectionBlackholedPeer, TestFaultedCarrierRefusesNewSessions,
TestNoGoroutineLeakAfterMixedWorkload, TestSoakMixedSessionsUnderFaults.
